+# Movement 002 review — simulated cold read
+
+This is a **fresh-context simulated cold read**, not a real audience measurement. I read only the six supplied manuscript chapters, Chapters 7–12, in order. I had no canon packet or drafting history. The two reader perspectives below are editorial lenses, not demographic research or a sales forecast.
+
+The supplied movement appears complete as a six-chapter unit, although it intentionally ends one morning before Cael's promised ruling.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+My interest first caught on the concrete danger of the thawed river, then caught decisively at the Chapter 7 board scene when Seln explains that the apparently mild gate can freeze the crew's standing and send Cael back to a frame. The phrase "To a frame" gives the institutional threat a human body. From there, Lira's decision to use both hands, her bout with Cael, and the first ferry crossing form a clean rising line. The ferry failure is the movement's strongest suspense sequence: every solution grows from an already visible skill, injury, tool, or local fact. Vastin making himself into an anchor without any declared power is an especially satisfying entrance.
+
+Interest slipped in two places. The opening pages require the reader to absorb ferry mechanics, the day count, several names, and the delayed crossing before the crew's personal stakes surface. Later, across Chapters 10–11, the distinctions among office, seat, seal, instrument, restriction, function, layer, and archive arrive in a long run of exact dialogue. The scene remains intelligent and emotionally alive, but a cold reader can lose the current decision while following the institutional vocabulary.
+
+Lira matters most to me in this movement. Her left hand is simultaneously injury, shame, tool, tactic, and proof that she is still one person rather than "two sorts of girl." Vastin becomes nearly as important within a single crossing because his habits survive the loss of his office, and because the story lets his empty hands hurt more than a speech about lost status would.
+
+I expect the next event to be Cael asking for hands at the plank and ruling on Treswick. I also expect Oryn to ask, rather than assume, whether she may examine Vastin's knee; Seln's recognition of the technician's date and Oryn's repeatedly swallowed sentence feel like the next two disclosures waiting behind that decision.
+
+I would continue without hesitation. The movement pays off the boat, Lira's recovery work, Cael's practice, Seln's count, and Vastin's earlier moral choice while opening a specific next road rather than merely enlarging the mystery.
+
+## Fluent thirteen-year-old reader lens
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 7/10 | Chapter 7, opening launch, from "The scow went into the river stern first" through the ferryman's first crossing forecast | The moving scow and dangerous thaw are concrete, but the personal reason to care does not fully arrive until Seln's count and Cael's delayed ruling. | High |
+| Keep reading | 9/10 | Chapter 12, final store-room exchange, from "You said that you'd rule when the boat was in" to the rope going around the second box | The morning vote, the prepared boxes, Seln's date recognition, and Oryn's unasked question create strong forward pressure after substantial local payoffs. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 9, ferry rescue, from "It was a raft of shelf ice" through Vastin holding the stern strap | A ferry accident is solved through soil, buried timber, animal power, knots, and an old man's judgment; the problem feels particular rather than interchangeable. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 7/10 | Chapter 10, noon-board explanation, from "I hold no office" through "There is no key" | The social action is clear, but the nested distinctions among the frame, function, plate, deep layer, schedules, office, and archive demand rereading without prior context. | Medium-high |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 8, the bout, from "Don't you stop!" through Lira taking Cael's offered help with her left wrist | Lira chooses the risk, Cael must overcome protective habit, and the recovery is neither painless nor magical. Their care shows through what each permits the other to do. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 7, the stool scene beginning "That's a seat"; Chapter 10, "It's the mule's" / "Then I shall not presume upon it" | The mule's chair becomes a running joke that also measures Oryn's distance from the group. Brom's literal gravity and Vastin's procedural response make the warmth character-specific. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 9/10 | Chapter 9, from the stern strap parting to "The scow's bow struck the landing" | Position, load, current, failing equipment, and available help stay trackable. Each adjustment changes the problem, and the twenty-two-minute recovery feels earned rather than extended. | High |
+| Progression payoff | 9/10 | Chapter 8, Lira's final feint: "It can't hit you yet. It can make you look at it"; Chapter 9, Cael reading the post's footing | Practice produces new usable choices. Lira turns slowness into deception, while Cael rejects flashier fragments and applies a limited read to the exact terrain problem. | High |
+| Connection | 9/10 | Chapter 7's woven hurdle and both-hands promise; Chapter 9's two-handed knot; Chapter 12's return to the promised ruling | Objects, injuries, counts, debts, and spoken promises recur with changed meaning. The movement rewards remembering small practical details. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 7/10 | Chapter 9, the long paragraph beginning "It stood at the landing's head"; Chapter 12, the explanation beginning "It is a technician's return" | The cadence and dialogue attribution are strong, but several long sentences stack spatial or institutional clauses that a listener cannot look back over. | Medium-high |
+
+For this lens, the chief accessibility issue is not the old-fashioned diction itself. The concrete words—rope, post, bowl, hand, leaf—usually make that diction legible. Difficulty rises when several unseen bureaucratic layers are distinguished in succession without a renewed statement of what choice those distinctions change.
+
